# How much of the world's energy still comes from fossil fuels?
subtitle: "Two hosts argue over the fossil slice of the world's energy pie, then find why it has barely shrunk since 1990 even as solar power boomed."
tags: ["earth","economics","data"]
format: quiz
thumb: "band \"Solar boomed. And yet…\" note \"1990 → 2025\""
voices: {"a":"gemini:Schedar | an even, thoughtful data-journalist quiz host; calm, natural pace","b":"gemini:Pulcherrima | a forward co-host who says what most people think; natural, never exaggerated","take":"lines"}

## How much of the world's energy still comes from fossil fuels?
use: pie_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Fossil
      - Clean
    values:
      - 86.2
      - 13.8
    label_size: 34
sources:
    - id: owid_share
      title: Share of primary energy from fossil fuels
      authors: Our World in Data, from Energy Institute Statistical Review of World Energy (2026) and Smil (2017)
      year: 2026
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/fossil-fuels-share-energy
      finding: 'fossil fuels'' share of total energy supply (OWID''s current chart, Energy Institute data released 2026, which now includes 2025): 90.3% in 1990, 86.2% in 2025. OWID''s older substitution-method series gave about 81%; this cast uses the chart as OWID publishes it today.'
    - id: owid_twh
      title: Fossil fuel consumption
      authors: Our World in Data, from Energy Institute Statistical Review of World Energy (2026)
      year: 2026
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/fossil-fuel-primary-energy
      finding: world fossil fuel use rose from about 83,000 TWh in 1990 to about 144,000 TWh in 2025 (+73%)
    - id: owid_solar
      title: Solar power generation
      authors: Our World in Data, from Ember and Energy Institute (2026)
      year: 2026
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/solar-energy-consumption
      finding: world solar generation rose from 32 TWh in 2010 to about 2,780 TWh in 2025

    icon drum size 170 set twemoji x 230 y 430 icon_look picture of "oil drum" hidden true
    icon fire size 170 set twemoji x 500 y 430 icon_look picture of factory hidden true
    icon plane size 170 set twemoji x 770 y 430 icon_look picture of airplane hidden true
    icon sun size 100 set twemoji x 440 y 640 icon_look picture of sun hidden true
    text half "half?" #8f887c x 500 y 265 font_size 72 hidden true
    sector p90_f #b5482e x 215 y 440 radius 134 start -235.07999999999998 end 90 style.fill #b5482e style.fill_style hatch hidden true
    sector p90_c #2f6b8f x 215 y 440 radius 134 start 90 end 124.92000000000002 style.fill #2f6b8f style.fill_style hatch hidden true
    group p90 members ["p90_f","p90_c"] hidden true
    sector p25_f #b5482e x 690 y 440 radius 180 start -220.32 end 90 style.fill #b5482e style.fill_style hatch hidden true
    sector p25_c #2f6b8f x 690 y 440 radius 180 start 90 end 139.68 style.fill #2f6b8f style.fill_style hatch hidden true
    group p25 members ["p25_f","p25_c"] hidden true
    text l90 "1990: 90%" #8f887c cites ["owid_share"] x 215 y 255 font_size 48 hidden true
    text l25 "2025: 86%" #b5482e cites ["owid_share"] x 690 y 215 font_size 48 hidden true
    text x85 "solar ×85" #2f6b8f cites ["owid_solar"] x 440 y 560 font_size 40 hidden true

    card title "How much energy is still fossil?"

A: How much of the world's energy still comes from coal, oil and gas? Count everything: power plants, factories, cars, planes and heating.
    draw drum fire plane parallel true

B: Surely not that much any more. There are solar panels everywhere. Half?
    draw half

    erase drum fire plane half

    ask "Drag the edge of the slice to your guess." on slice_1 store g default "50"
        right: Close: about eighty-six percent.
        wrong: It's about eighty-six percent: still most of everything.

B: Eighty-six percent? In 2025? That can't be right.
    highlight slice_1

    erase slice_1 slice_2

    draw p25 l25

A: It is. In 1990 it was ninety, so in thirty-five years the fossil slice shrank by just four points.
    draw p90 l90

A: And that's despite a solar boom: solar power has grown about eighty-five times over since 2010.
    draw sun x85

B: So clean energy is winning?
    highlight p25_c

    erase sun x85

A: Not yet. Look at the size of the pies: the world now uses about eighty percent more energy than in 1990.
    highlight p25 effect circle

A: So a slightly smaller slice of a much bigger pie is still more fossil fuel: about seventy percent more.
    arrow grow #b5482e from.x 360 from.y 440 to.x 495 to.y 440
    text more "+73%" #b5482e cites ["owid_twh"] x 428 y 490 font_size 50
    text more2 "fossil" #b5482e x 428 y 395 font_size 34

B: So clean energy has mostly fed the new demand, not replaced coal and oil.
    highlight p25_c

A: Exactly. That's the real race: clean energy has to grow faster than demand.
    highlight more

